description:
pkix-chain/src/lib.rs:693-708 (Verifier::verify_batch):

    /// This is a sequential loop over [`Verifier::verify_one`]; the
    /// chains do not share any per-validation state. Callers requiring
    /// cross-chain caching (memoised path-builder candidates,
    /// revocation lookups, etc.) should layer that on top of
    /// `verify_one` or inside their [`SignatureVerifier`] /
    /// [`RevocationChecker`] implementations.
    pub fn verify_batch(&self, chains: &[&[Certificate]]) -> Vec<crate::Result<ValidatedPath>> {
        chains.iter().map(|chain| self.verify_one(chain)).collect()
    }

The rustdoc explicitly commits to "sequential loop" semantics. That word locks in two contract clauses:

1. **Ordering visibility.** Side-effecting callback implementations (a SignatureVerifier that logs each invocation; an AiaFetcher that batches HTTP requests in a connection pool; a RevocationChecker that emits metrics) observe their callbacks in chain[0]-first, chain[N-1]-last order. A caller relying on observed ordering for diagnostics or batching is depending on the API.

2. **Error-isolation contract.** "failures in one chain do not abort the others" is documented (line 696). The Vec<Result> shape ALREADY communicates this. The "sequential" addition pins ordering BEYOND error-isolation.

WHY IT MATTERS:

AGENTS.md non-negotiable #6 names "Batch APIs where setup cost is non-trivial" as a supported pattern. The future-proof shape for batch is rayon-able: `chains.par_iter().map(...).collect()` with the same `Vec<Result>` output. The current rustdoc says that is **not** what verify_batch does. A future contributor who tries to flip to par_iter for performance has to either:

- Break the documented "sequential" promise (and any caller depending on observed ordering); or
- Add a NEW method (verify_batch_parallel?) and live with both forever.

The actual implementation is "map over a slice and collect" — there's no semantic reason it has to be sequential. The doc commitment is the only thing preventing a non-breaking switch.

The docstring also says "the chains do not share any per-validation state" which IS a real constraint (callbacks must be Send+Sync — which PKIX-sqek.8 separately addresses). That part is sound.

OPTIONS:

(a) Drop the word "sequential" — say "the chains are verified independently; the result Vec has the same length and order as the input." That preserves the order-of-results contract (which is necessary; callers consume by index) while leaving evaluation order unspecified. Future parallel evaluation is non-breaking.

(b) Keep "sequential" and live with the lock-in. Acceptable if you genuinely intend verify_batch to remain a thin wrapper for simple cases, and a future `verify_batch_parallel` (or `Verifier::with_parallelism(n).verify_batch(...)`) is the parallel path.

(c) Explicitly document "evaluation order is unspecified and may run in parallel" — most flexible, but requires callers to NOT lean on observation order even today.

This is a SOFT immutability issue — the function signature isn't locked, just one descriptive word in the rustdoc. But once shipped at 1.0, callers will write code that depends on the word, and removing it becomes a soft-breaking change ("we used to promise this and now we don't"). Cheap to fix pre-1.0.
